WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.980
Instructor: So the next step, a very important one,

00:01.980 --> 00:04.260
is to register for GitHub.

00:04.260 --> 00:07.380
So go to github.com if you don't have an account yet

00:07.380 --> 00:08.733
and click Sign up.

00:09.720 --> 00:10.830
So then, you'll need to come up

00:10.830 --> 00:14.610
with a username, an email address, and a password.

00:14.610 --> 00:17.250
Make sure that you use a real email address

00:17.250 --> 00:19.950
because you will need to verify the email.

00:19.950 --> 00:21.510
And it's helpful,

00:21.510 --> 00:25.260
things work better if the email address you use here

00:25.260 --> 00:29.040
is the one that you configured very early on in the course.

00:29.040 --> 00:31.050
If you remember, we did this,

00:31.050 --> 00:34.803
let's see, git config user.email.

00:35.701 --> 00:40.080
So I'm actually using a special email that GitHub gives me,

00:40.080 --> 00:43.800
but basically we want git to use the same email address here

00:43.800 --> 00:45.720
that we use on GitHub.

00:45.720 --> 00:48.270
I use this weird noreply email

00:48.270 --> 00:50.880
just so that I'm not like publicly blasting my email address

00:50.880 --> 00:54.870
in every git commit or in every video I do using git.

00:54.870 --> 00:59.130
But it works best if whatever your account email is

00:59.130 --> 01:02.160
is also set up locally in your git config,

01:02.160 --> 01:05.610
and that just helps GitHub know who you are, you know.

01:05.610 --> 01:06.990
When you push up code,

01:06.990 --> 01:09.690
it knows to use your profile and your image

01:09.690 --> 01:12.150
and sort of track your work.

01:12.150 --> 01:15.600
Anyway, so sign up, use an email address, your username.

01:15.600 --> 01:18.030
And then the second step, once that is done,

01:18.030 --> 01:20.250
is to configure an SSH key.

01:20.250 --> 01:22.650
So without going into too much detail,

01:22.650 --> 01:27.480
SSH, I believe stands for Secure Shell, is this protocol

01:27.480 --> 01:31.200
that allows us to be authenticated without having to write

01:31.200 --> 01:33.630
our email or our username and password

01:33.630 --> 01:36.090
every single time we wanna interact with GitHub

01:36.090 --> 01:37.860
from the command line.

01:37.860 --> 01:42.330
So what we need to do is generate one of these SSH keys

01:42.330 --> 01:44.460
and then tell GitHub about it.

01:44.460 --> 01:45.390
And to do that,

01:45.390 --> 01:48.200
we're gonna follow the instructions on GitHub

01:48.200 --> 01:50.490
that go into detail about how to do that.

01:50.490 --> 01:53.490
So it depends on the platform you're on, but the first step

01:53.490 --> 01:57.300
is to check if you have an SSH key generated already,

01:57.300 --> 02:01.544
and all you do to figure that out is run this command here.

02:01.544 --> 02:03.270
You can just copy this command.

02:03.270 --> 02:04.770
You don't want that dollar sign.

02:04.770 --> 02:06.930
And this is going to show a whole bunch of,

02:06.930 --> 02:09.960
it may show a whole bunch of existing files

02:09.960 --> 02:12.547
in a directory called .ssh.

02:12.547 --> 02:14.310
If nothing is in there

02:14.310 --> 02:17.130
or, rather, if you don't see any of these,

02:17.130 --> 02:19.080
id_rsa.pub,

02:19.080 --> 02:21.420
id_ecdca.pub,

02:21.420 --> 02:23.430
these are all different hashing algorithms.

02:23.430 --> 02:24.660
If you don't see one of these

02:24.660 --> 02:26.490
or you just wanna make a new key,

02:26.490 --> 02:28.050
if you're brand new to all these,

02:28.050 --> 02:30.150
you likely don't have any of these files.

02:30.150 --> 02:33.990
It's no big deal. All we need to do is generate a new key.

02:33.990 --> 02:35.670
So this is where things diverge a bit

02:35.670 --> 02:38.130
if you're on a Windows machine versus a Mac.

02:38.130 --> 02:40.320
I don't think the differences are that significant,

02:40.320 --> 02:45.300
but definitely do it following the tutorial here on GitHub.

02:45.300 --> 02:47.580
Don't use this video as the source of truth.

02:47.580 --> 02:48.810
Things change quickly.

02:48.810 --> 02:51.450
I'll include it as one of the resources.

02:51.450 --> 02:52.950
So I'll go through this process.

02:52.950 --> 02:55.800
I've already set up an SSH key, but I'll do the same thing.

02:55.800 --> 02:58.590
We're gonna copy this right here,

02:58.590 --> 03:00.330
and then we put our email in there,

03:00.330 --> 03:02.970
and that will act as our label.

03:02.970 --> 03:05.301
So I'm gonna go to my terminal.

03:05.301 --> 03:06.840
I'm gonna run this,

03:06.840 --> 03:09.810
then in quotes just gonna use this email here.

03:09.810 --> 03:12.077
This is the one I use for git and GitHub.

03:13.050 --> 03:15.720
Okay, and then it's gonna ask me for a file

03:15.720 --> 03:17.730
where it should save that key.

03:17.730 --> 03:20.940
So this is the default filename and the default location,

03:20.940 --> 03:23.310
in this .ssh folder.

03:23.310 --> 03:25.920
If you just hit Enter, it's going to use that.

03:25.920 --> 03:28.920
You can also come up with your own filename if you want.

03:28.920 --> 03:33.883
However, all the GitHub tutorials use this name, id_ed25519.

03:35.520 --> 03:38.730
So I'm just gonna go with that. I'm gonna hit Enter.

03:38.730 --> 03:40.620
And then enter a passphrase.

03:40.620 --> 03:42.000
So you can type that passphrase,

03:42.000 --> 03:44.610
and then enter the same passphrase again.

03:44.610 --> 03:45.960
All right, so then it tells me

03:45.960 --> 03:49.950
that my identification has been saved in this file

03:49.950 --> 03:52.800
and my public key has been saved in a similar file,

03:52.800 --> 03:54.570
but it ends in .pub.

03:54.570 --> 03:55.830
It's the public key.

03:55.830 --> 03:57.870
I'm not going into detail, explaining how this stuff works

03:57.870 --> 04:00.330
because it doesn't really matter at this point.

04:00.330 --> 04:01.950
We just wanna get it set up.

04:01.950 --> 04:03.330
So the next step

04:03.330 --> 04:07.260
is to then add the SSH key to the SSH agent.

04:07.260 --> 04:09.780
So we're gonna copy this line, if you're on a Mac,

04:09.780 --> 04:11.190
and there are some instructions.

04:11.190 --> 04:13.650
Depending on the macOS operating system,

04:13.650 --> 04:15.210
I guess that's what OS is,

04:15.210 --> 04:17.340
and depending on the version that you're running,

04:17.340 --> 04:19.800
you may need to make some additional changes.

04:19.800 --> 04:21.654
So I'm gonna try this here.

04:21.654 --> 04:24.780
Okay, so that looks like it's working fine.

04:24.780 --> 04:27.150
So the next step on a Mac is to check

04:27.150 --> 04:30.930
if we have this file called config in the ssh directory.

04:30.930 --> 04:32.580
So we can see if it exists.

04:32.580 --> 04:33.780
And if it doesn't exist,

04:33.780 --> 04:35.700
you can run this command to make it.

04:35.700 --> 04:38.700
So if you get this line, it says it does not exist,

04:38.700 --> 04:40.950
try this one to make that file,

04:40.950 --> 04:44.100
and, again, this is only if you're on a newer Mac.

04:44.100 --> 04:46.800
And then here, we need to open that file

04:46.800 --> 04:49.560
and then put this right here, just copy this,

04:49.560 --> 04:51.600
and put that in the file.

04:51.600 --> 04:54.660
Now, if you gave your filename, when you generated the keys,

04:54.660 --> 04:56.850
if you gave it a different name from the default,

04:56.850 --> 04:59.820
you'll need to reflect that name here, but I'm just going

04:59.820 --> 05:01.710
with what they have in the documentation.

05:01.710 --> 05:02.940
So I'm gonna paste that in,

05:02.940 --> 05:06.510
hopefully you can see this, and save.

05:06.510 --> 05:08.130
So that was the name of my file.

05:08.130 --> 05:10.350
If you remember, right here,

05:10.350 --> 05:12.930
this is what I set to save it as it just hit Enter.

05:12.930 --> 05:17.220
So that matches this right here coming from the GitHub Docs.

05:17.220 --> 05:20.636
Also, if you elected to use a passcode

05:20.636 --> 05:23.430
when you generated the key, you want this line.

05:23.430 --> 05:26.460
If you did not use a passcode, you don't want that line.

05:26.460 --> 05:28.200
Now, the final step here

05:28.200 --> 05:31.050
is, well, the final step before we go to GitHub

05:31.050 --> 05:34.710
is to copy this line which is to add our key

05:34.710 --> 05:37.980
to something called the SSH agent which we just started up.

05:37.980 --> 05:40.080
And if we do this, again, you need to make sure

05:40.080 --> 05:41.820
you're using the correct filename

05:41.820 --> 05:44.730
that matches the filename you generated,

05:44.730 --> 05:46.560
but, again, this is the default.

05:46.560 --> 05:49.620
So if you didn't deviate, this is all you need to do.

05:49.620 --> 05:53.760
We'll run this line. Okay, identity added.

05:53.760 --> 05:56.670
So now, final step is to go to GitHub

05:56.670 --> 05:59.733
and add that SSH key to the GitHub account.

06:00.600 --> 06:03.300
So what we need to do is copy the contents

06:03.300 --> 06:06.420
of the key that was generated, the public key.

06:06.420 --> 06:07.860
So, again, just copy this line

06:07.860 --> 06:10.983
if you're using the same name and you didn't modify it.

06:11.893 --> 06:15.946
You won't see any output, but this command, pbcopy,

06:15.946 --> 06:19.620
copies the output of this into your clipboard.

06:19.620 --> 06:23.010
So on the clipboard right now I have this key here.

06:23.010 --> 06:25.470
So I got that. I have access to it.

06:25.470 --> 06:28.320
And now I need to go onto GitHub

06:28.320 --> 06:32.040
and go to my profile, profile photo up top,

06:32.040 --> 06:35.310
and then go to Settings, I believe is what it is.

06:35.310 --> 06:37.200
Super zoomed in here. Here we are.

06:37.200 --> 06:41.190
And then I click on SSH, and then I'll add a new key.

06:41.190 --> 06:44.430
So I'll just demonstrate this. I go to GitHub.

06:44.430 --> 06:47.850
Here's my profile, I'm gonna go to Settings,

06:47.850 --> 06:50.760
and then SSH and GPG keys,

06:50.760 --> 06:54.930
and then I'm gonna click New SSH key just like that,

06:54.930 --> 06:56.380
then I'm gonna paste this in,

06:57.300 --> 06:59.820
thing I copied from the clipboard over here

06:59.820 --> 07:01.500
or copied to my clipboard.

07:01.500 --> 07:03.000
And then give it a title

07:03.000 --> 07:07.833
just like laptop, for example, or mykey, it doesn't matter,

07:09.596 --> 07:11.760
and I'm going to add it.

07:11.760 --> 07:14.387
All right, so we should be good to go now.

07:14.387 --> 07:18.600
I know it's obnoxious process, but once we have it done,

07:18.600 --> 07:20.400
